    <div class="entry-content" set:html={`<p><a href="/product-category/sesame-series/">What is Black Sesame?</a> It is a black seed from the sesame plant in the Pedaliaceae family, also known as hu ma, ju sheng, or you ma. It is an annual herbaceous plant. Due to its rich nutritional and medicinal properties, black sesame is predominantly used in the food industry. Below, we will provide a detailed introduction.</p><p><img src="/images/图片11-300x300.jpg" decoding="async" class="alignnone wp-image-446" alt="what is black sesame" width="536" height="536" title="What is Black Sesame? 10 Facts Good to Know 4" /></p><div id="ez-toc-container" class="ez-toc-v2_0_85 counter-hierarchy ez-toc-counter ez-toc-grey ez-toc-container-direction"><div class="ez-toc-title-container"><p class="ez-toc-title" style="cursor:inherit">Table of Contents</p>
<span class="ez-toc-title-toggle"><a href="#" class="ez-toc-pull-right ez-toc-btn ez-toc-btn-xs ez-toc-btn-default ez-toc-toggle" aria-label="Toggle Table of Content"><span class="ez-toc-js-icon-con"><span class=""><span class="eztoc-hide" style="display:none;">Toggle</span><span class="ez-toc-icon-toggle-span"><svg style="fill: #999;color:#999" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="list-377408" width="20px" height="20px" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M6 6H4v2h2V6zm14 0H8v2h12V6zM4 11h2v2H4v-2zm16 0H8v2h12v-2zM4 16h2v2H4v-2zm16 0H8v2h12v-2z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg><svg style="fill: #999;color:#999" class="arrow-unsorted-368013" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="10px" height="10px" viewBox="0 0 24 24" version="1.2" baseProfile="tiny"><path d="M18.2 9.3l-6.2-6.3-6.2 6.3c-.2.2-.3.4-.3.7s.1.5.3.7c.2.2.4.3.7.3h11c.3 0 .5-.1.7-.3.2-.2.3-.5.3-.7s-.1-.5-.3-.7zM5.8 14.7l6.2 6.3 6.2-6.3c.2-.2.3-.5.3-.7s-.1-.5-.3-.7c-.2-.2-.4-.3-.7-.3h-11c-.3 0-.5.1-.7.3-.2.2-.3.5-.3.7s.1.5.3.7z"/></svg></span></span></span></a></span></div><nav><ul class='ez-toc-list ez-toc-list-level-1 ' ><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-1" href="#Are_all_uses_of_black_sesame_in_the_food_industry_the_same" >Are all uses of black sesame in the food industry the same?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-2" href="#Which_type_of_black_sesame_is_more_suitable_for_food_production" >Which type of black sesame is more suitable for food production?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-3" href="#Do_foods_made_from_black_sesame_taste_the_same_as_those_made_from_white_sesame" >Do foods made from black sesame taste the same as those made from white sesame?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-4" href="#What_is_the_processing_technology_for_black_sesame" >What is the processing technology for black sesame?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-5" href="#What_are_the_uses_of_black_sesame" >What are the uses of black sesame?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-6" href="#Is_Black_Sesame_Nutritionally_Valuable_Compared_to_White_Sesame" >Is Black Sesame Nutritionally Valuable Compared to White Sesame?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-7" href="#How_to_Choose_High-Quality_Black_Sesame" >How to Choose High-Quality Black Sesame?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-8" href="#Should_You_Choose_Shelled_or_Unshelled_Black_Sesame" >Should You Choose Shelled or Unshelled Black Sesame?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-9" href="#Can_Black_Sesame_Be_Used_for_Oil_Extraction" >Can Black Sesame Be Used for Oil Extraction?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-10" href="#Which_Is_Better_for_Oil_Extraction_Black_or_White_Sesame" >Which Is Better for Oil Extraction: Black or White Sesame?</a></li></ul></nav></div><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Are_all_uses_of_black_sesame_in_the_food_industry_the_same"></span>Are all uses of black sesame in the food industry the same?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>China profoundly understands food, and the same ingredient can be served in various forms. Black sesame is no exception.</p><p>Specifically, black sesame is commonly used to make various food products such as black sesame paste, black sesame cookies, black sesame brittle, and black sesame candy, cherished by consumers for its unique taste and nutritional value. It also has applications in traditional Chinese medicine, mainly for its medicinal benefits, including nourishing the liver and kidneys and moisturizing dryness.</p><p>Thus, the uses of black sesame in the food industry are diverse.</p><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Which_type_of_black_sesame_is_more_suitable_for_food_production"></span>Which type of black sesame is more suitable for food production?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>The flavor of black sesame can vary depending on its origin, influencing the types of food it is used to produce.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Origin</th><th>Characteristics</th><th>Suitable Foods</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Central Plains Black Sesame</td><td>Plump grains, bright color, delicate and non-greasy texture</td><td>Black sesame paste, sesame sauce</td></tr><tr><td>Heilongjiang Black Sesame</td><td>Larger grains, higher oil content</td><td>Stir-frying, fillings, black sesame paste, desserts like osmanthus jelly</td></tr><tr><td>Taiyuan Black Sesame</td><td>Smaller, uniform grains with rich aroma and taste</td><td>Baking, pastry making</td></tr></tbody></table><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Do_foods_made_from_black_sesame_taste_the_same_as_those_made_from_white_sesame"></span>Do foods made from black sesame taste the same as those made from white sesame?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>There is a significant difference in taste between foods made from black sesame and those made from white sesame. Black sesame usually has a richer aroma and a slightly bitter flavor, making it popular among those who enjoy strong flavors. Foods made from black sesame, such as black sesame paste and black sesame tangyuan (sweet rice balls), have a pronounced sesame fragrance and a rich taste.</p><p>In contrast, white sesame has a more delicate aroma and a milder taste. It is commonly used to make sesame sauce and sesame candy, with its light flavor making it suitable for various snacks and pastries.</p><p>Therefore, when cooking and preparing food, individuals can choose between black and white sesame based on their flavor preferences and specific needs, creating a wider variety of unique and delicious dishes.</p><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="What_is_the_processing_technology_for_black_sesame"></span>What is the processing technology for black sesame?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>The detailed processing methods for black sesame are as follows:</p><ul><li><strong>Roasting</strong>: This traditional method involves frying black sesame in a pan to make it fragrant and crispy, helping to release its nutrients for better absorption by the body. The temperature and time must be strictly controlled to avoid burning or nutrient loss.</li><li><strong>Low-temperature Baking</strong>: This gentler processing method preserves more nutrients and natural flavors by baking black sesame at lower temperatures. While it usually takes longer, it ensures better texture and quality.</li><li><strong>Extrusion and Expansion</strong>: A modern processing technique that uses mechanical extrusion and high temperature and pressure to quickly expand black sesame, creating a crunchy product. While this enhances convenience and taste, it may affect its nutritional content.</li><li><strong>Low-temperature Crushing</strong>: This method targets extracting oil from black sesame by crushing and pressing it under low-temperature conditions, preserving the nutritional components and natural flavors of the oil while preventing high temperatures from degrading its quality.</li></ul><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="What_are_the_uses_of_black_sesame"></span>What are the uses of black sesame?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>Black sesame has many uses, including the production of black sesame paste, black sesame candy, and black sesame oil. It can also serve as a baking ingredient for making pastries and bread. In traditional Chinese medicine, it is noted for its benefits in nourishing the liver and kidneys and moisturizing dryness.</p><p>Notably, black sesame oil is produced through a traditional method that extracts oil from black sesame, resulting in a product with a unique aroma and nutritional value.</p><p>Black sesame oil is dark in color, aromatic, and has a distinct sesame flavor. It is rich in unsaturated fatty acids, vitamin E, and minerals, offering high nutritional value.</p><ul><li><strong>Unsaturated Fatty Acids</strong>: Help lower cholesterol and triglyceride levels in the blood, maintaining lipid balance.</li><li><strong>Vitamin E</strong>: Exhibits excellent antioxidant properties, clearing free radicals from the body and delaying aging.</li><li><strong>Minerals</strong>: Such as calcium, magnesium, and iron, play positive roles in bone health and blood pressure regulation.</li></ul><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Is_Black_Sesame_Nutritionally_Valuable_Compared_to_White_Sesame"></span>Is Black Sesame Nutritionally Valuable Compared to White Sesame?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>Black sesame is highly nutritious, containing significant amounts of fat, protein, carbohydrates, vitamins A and E, lecithin, calcium, iron, and more. It offers benefits such as lowering blood pressure, promoting hair health, enhancing skin appearance, and improving fertility. Compared to white sesame, black sesame excels in certain aspects: it has 1.43 times more insoluble dietary fiber, 1.26 times more calcium, and 1.33 times more vitamin E. Additionally, black sesame is rich in anthocyanins, which possess strong antioxidant properties, helping to eliminate free radicals and protect cardiovascular health.</p><p>Given its high nutritional value, black sesame can be easily incorporated into daily life. Here are some ways to utilize it:</p><ul><li><strong>Direct Consumption</strong>: Enjoy black sesame as a snack for quick and convenient nutrition.</li><li><strong>Cooking Ingredient</strong>: Add black sesame to dishes like porridge, stir-fries, or soups to enhance flavor and nutrition.</li><li><strong>Black Sesame Oil</strong>: Extract oil through pressing or infusion for cooking, salad dressings, or sauces.</li><li><strong>Combining with Other Ingredients</strong>: Pair black sesame with nutritious foods like dates, walnuts, or oats for enhanced nutritional benefits.</li><li><strong>Making Black Sesame Powder</strong>: Grind black sesame into powder to add to milk, soy milk, or yogurt, or use in baking for a richer taste.</li></ul><p><img src="/images/图片21-300x300.jpg" decoding="async" class="alignnone wp-image-447" alt="what is black sesame" width="568" height="568" title="What is Black Sesame? 10 Facts Good to Know 5" /></p><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="How_to_Choose_High-Quality_Black_Sesame"></span>How to Choose High-Quality Black Sesame?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>When selecting quality black sesame, consider the following four aspects:</p><ol><li><strong>Origin</strong>: Quality black sesame is primarily produced in provinces like Shaanxi, Gansu, and Henan in China, as well as in regions such as India, Ethiopia, Hawaii, Australia, Japan, Canada, Brazil, Thailand, Vietnam, Korea, and Madagascar.</li><li><strong>Appearance</strong>: Look for uniform color (dark gray to black), plump and intact seeds without broken or insect-damaged grains. The inside should be white when cut.</li><li><strong>Smell</strong>: Fresh black sesame has a distinct aroma. If it smells off or lacks fragrance, it may be stale or mixed with impurities.</li><li><strong>Taste</strong>: Quality black sesame should have a mild sweetness and fragrance. If it tastes bitter or flavorless, it may be of low quality or dyed.</li></ol><p>You can also check if the sesame leaves a mark when rubbed in your palm or if it discolors in water—true black sesame will not turn the water black but rather a tea color.</p><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Should_You_Choose_Shelled_or_Unshelled_Black_Sesame"></span>Should You Choose Shelled or Unshelled Black Sesame?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>Most black sesame found in stores is unshelled, which retains more nutrients like lignans and has a crunchier texture with a richer flavor. Shelled black sesame has higher sugar content and lower fiber, making it commonly used for cooking or as a snack. The choice depends on personal preference for nutrition versus taste.</p><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Can_Black_Sesame_Be_Used_for_Oil_Extraction"></span>Can Black Sesame Be Used for Oil Extraction?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>Yes, black sesame can be used for oil extraction due to its high oil content and unique aroma. Benefits of black sesame oil include:</p><ul><li><strong>Blood Pressure Reduction</strong>: Rich in magnesium, it helps lower blood pressure.</li><li><strong>Digestive Aid</strong>: Supports digestion and absorption.</li><li><strong>Constipation Relief</strong>: Enhances bowel movements.</li><li><strong>Skin Health</strong>: Contains vitamin E, offering antioxidant benefits.</li><li><strong>Hair Care</strong>: Nourishes hair and promotes melanin production.</li><li><strong>Nutritional Support</strong>: Rich in iron and vitamin E, it helps prevent anemia.</li><li><strong>Cognitive Benefits</strong>: High in unsaturated fatty acids, it supports brain health.</li></ul><h2><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Which_Is_Better_for_Oil_Extraction_Black_or_White_Sesame"></span>Which Is Better for Oil Extraction: Black or White Sesame?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p>Generally, white sesame is preferred for oil extraction due to its thinner shell, allowing easier separation and higher oil quality. It also provides better economic returns. In contrast, black sesame yields less oil due to its tougher shell but contains more protein and trace elements, making it beneficial for health. Its rich aroma and flavor make black sesame oil suitable for upscale dining.</p><p>Note: The nutritional and medicinal values of black sesame referenced are sourced from &#8220;Compendium of Materia Medica,&#8221; &#8220;Shennong Bencao Jing,&#8221; and &#8220;Ming Yi Lu.&#8221;</p>`} />
